Task: Suggest availability from existing rental periods (Phase 2, Tier 1/2)
Track: fy_tax_pack_20260729
INSERTED TASK - user challenged the design at the checkpoint: "we do also
already capture rental periods in the system. I wonder if storing these
separately is a good idea or if we can derive it from the rental periods
that are already present?"

THE DESIGN QUESTION
Fair challenge: rental_periods already holds start_date/end_date, so
asking for availability dates again is duplicate entry and lets the two
drift apart.

But TENANCY IS NOT AVAILABILITY. rental_periods records when the property
was LET (it carries weekly_rent, management_fee_pct, an agent). The ATO
test for "available for rent" is being genuinely on the market, which:
  - can begin BEFORE the first tenant moves in (the user's own case:
    "available from 22/11/2025, it was listed earlier"), and
  - continues BETWEEN leases while the property is still advertised.

Deriving availability purely from tenancies would silently drop both,
understating days available and therefore the apportioned deduction -
erring against the taxpayer.

RESOLUTION (user chose): suggest, don't derive. rental_periods produces a
starting point; the stored dates remain the recorded fact. Common case is
one click; the "listed earlier" case stays expressible. This also matches
the "reduce data entry to review" principle in product-guidelines.md.

VACANCY GAPS (user chose): bridge and disclose. A property between tenants
is normally still on the market, so the span bridges gaps - but
bridgedDays is reported and shown in the UI, so a property genuinely
withdrawn can be corrected. Silent bridging would have been an
undisclosed assumption.

FILES
- lib/tax/fy.ts - suggestAvailabilityFromTenancies + AvailabilitySuggestion
- tests/lib/tax/fy.test.ts - 9 new tests (61 total)
- components/property-fy-facts-panel.tsx - suggestion row with one-click
  accept, hidden once the inputs match it
- app/(dashboard)/.../tax-report/page.tsx - computes it from rentalPeriods,
  which the page already fetched (no extra query)

EDGE CASES HANDLED
- Overlapping tenancies merged before counting, so an overlap cannot be
  mistaken for a negative gap.
- Adjacent tenancies (end + 1 day == next start) treated as contiguous,
  not a one-day vacancy.
- Tenancies clamped to the FY; those entirely outside it ignored.
- Open-ended tenancy runs to year end.
- Returns null when nothing overlaps, so no suggestion is offered rather
  than a misleading empty one.

